WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:03.960
You’ve probably heard of four states of
matter:

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:07.720
Solid, Liquid, Gas, and Plasma,

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:11.980
but there’s a fifth ultracold state that is
attracting a lot of attention:

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:16.940
the Bose-Einstein Condensate, discovered during the late 20th century,

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:20.480
and the object of intense research ever since.

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:24.280
This year, a condensate will make its way
to the space station.

00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:27.280
but why has this material suddenly rocketed to prominence,

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:30.660
and just what is a Bose-Einstein condensate?

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:35.880
BEC gets its name from the two scientists
who proposed its existence.

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:40.370
Satyendra Nath Bose and Albert Einstein.

00:00:40.370 --> 00:00:45.400
They suggested that when certain atoms are
cooled to temperatures near absolute zero,

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:50.180
the atoms lose their individual identities
and crowd into a single quantum state.

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:52.180
70 years after the proposal,

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:57.760
scientists finally achieved the temperatures needed to generate the condensate.

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:02.210
To get these atoms super cold, scientists use lasers.

00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:05.680
Lasers can be tuned so that atoms preferentially
absorb photons

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:08.080
when they move towards the laser.

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:12.140
Absorbing these laser photons slow down and cool the atoms.

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:14.820
Inside a vacuum chamber, an array of laser beams

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:17.020
cool a few atoms trapped within a magnetic field

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:21.140
to just fractions of a degree above absolute zero.

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:24.680
Just like Einstein predicted, when the temperatures
get this low,

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:28.480
regular physics seems to break down.

00:01:28.490 --> 00:01:32.490
This new matter forms into something called a superfluid.

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:35.380
Superfluids are fluids that have zero viscosity,

00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:39.560
meaning they flow without any friction or resistance.

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:43.240
Not only that, but a BEC can behave much like a light wave.

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:47.080
a BEC can overlap and interfere with itself

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:51.180
to spontaneously develop a rippled, wavy distribution of atoms.

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:54.180
Such wave-like effects are rare among forms
of matter,

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:57.680
so physicists are excited to study their intricacies.

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:02.940
But they only have milliseconds in which to
study the BECs, after they release the atoms

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:05.760
from their magnetic traps and gravity brings
them crashing

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:08.000
to the bottom of their vacuum chamber.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:11.640
Now, scientists want to send BECs to space,

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:16.040
where weightlessness gives them more time to perform experiments.

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:19.400
On Earth, the best way to mimic the weightlessness of space

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:22.760
is by performing experiments during free fall.

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:30.459
In Bremen, Germany, scientists are dropping
BEC experiments down a 146-meter tower.

00:02:30.459 --> 00:02:35.160
But they need to get the cold atoms into space
if they want to probe their BEC regularly

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:37.129
for more than a few seconds.

00:02:37.129 --> 00:02:43.560
The Cold Atom Laboratory is a remote controlled
device that will become operational in 2018,

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:47.550
after it is installed in the International
Space Station’s Destiny module.

00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:50.860
If all goes well, the CAL will be churning out data

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:55.220
for at least a year after it is switched on.

00:02:55.230 --> 00:02:59.640
Being in space also means they can cool atoms
to lower temperatures.

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:03.380
Scientists may reach record temperatures below
100 picokelvins:

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:06.800
colder than any spot in the universe.

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:12.280
Weightlessness also allows experiments to
happen that wouldn’t be possible otherwise.

00:03:12.290 --> 00:03:17.129
Some researchers are aiming to create BEC
bubbles, a form of the condensate that wouldn’t

00:03:17.129 --> 00:03:19.980
be possible back on Earth due to gravity.

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:25.849
Other scientists are looking into cooling
not just atoms, but individual molecules.

00:03:25.849 --> 00:03:31.280
While the space station is a better environment
than Earth for maintaining BECs, vibrations

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:35.900
from the machinery on board keep it from
being the perfect test bench.

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:38.480
In the future, space-based experiments

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:42.540
may be able to perform atom interferometry on the BECs.

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:48.820
Eventually, these atomic interferometers could
be launched on purpose-built satellites,

00:03:48.830 --> 00:03:53.260
and used to measure tiny variations in Earth’s gravity.

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:58.029
But for right now, there’s still a lot we
need to learn about this fifth state of matter.

